# CRITICAL findings that real, published Garry's Mod code is expected to
# produce. Everything here is a true positive: the construct really is in the
# file. They are listed rather than suppressed because a scanner that stays
# silent about RunString has stopped being a scanner.
#
# They fall into two groups. The first is stock game files and an admin mod's own
# privilege machinery. The second is three addons that run code on purpose --
# Starfall is a Lua sandbox, Wiremod's Expression 2 compiles chips, PAC3 builds
# parts from strings -- and every CRITICAL in them is the scanner correctly
# noticing that. A server owner who sees these in a scan has learned something
# true: this server can run code that did not ship with it.
#
# Format: <pattern id> <path below the checkout root>
# Adding a line here is a claim that you looked at the code and it is fine.
# real_corpus.sh fails on any CRITICAL that is not listed.
#
# CRITICAL findings are listed one by one above. Everything else is budgeted per
# rule instead, in the profile section at the end: how many findings a rule may
# produce on this corpus, and across how many files. A single ceiling over all
# HIGH findings used to do that job, but it could not say which rule had grown,
# and it went up every time a new rule was added for an unrelated reason.
#
# A rule that fires here at all needs a budget line. That is deliberate: writing
# one is the moment to look at what it matched and decide the rule is right.

# lua_run is the entity whose entire purpose is running Lua a mapper typed in.
LUA-001 garrysmod/garrysmod/gamemodes/base/entities/entities/lua_run.lua

# The main menu and the HTML panel run Lua the engine itself supplies.
LUA-001 garrysmod/garrysmod/lua/menu/mainmenu.lua
LUA-001 garrysmod/garrysmod/lua/vgui/dhtml.lua

# ULX's rcon command runs what a superadmin typed, which is what rcon is.
LUA-001 ulx/lua/ulx/modules/sh/rcon.lua

# The engine's own auth code assigns the listen-server host to superadmin.
LUA-040 garrysmod/garrysmod/lua/includes/extensions/player_auth.lua

# DarkRP's error library compiles a file on purpose, to turn a syntax error into
# a readable message instead of a stack trace.
LUA-002 darkrp/gamemode/libraries/simplerr.lua

# DarkRP really does enable sv_allowcslua on startup, unless the owner sets
# Config.disallowClientsideScripts. This is a true positive and arguably the
# most useful finding in the whole corpus: a server owner running DarkRP should
# know that clients may run their own Lua by default. It is listed rather than
# suppressed because the rule is right.
LUA-044 darkrp/gamemode/modules/base/sv_gamemode_functions.lua

# ULX's own admin panel. This is the XGUI screen where an admin clicks a player
# and picks a group, so it really does call "ulx adduserid" -- with values read
# out of the UI, not with a SteamID written into the file. That distinction is
# what COMP-024 tests, and COMP-024 correctly stays quiet here. Every backdoor
# in the malicious corpus that uses this call hardcodes the ID instead.
LUA-121 ulx/lua/ulx/xgui/groups.lua

# Starfall is a sandbox for user-written Lua. Compiling a string is not a smell
# here, it is the product: the editor validates, the instance loads, and the
# library hands the compiled chunk back. It reads files from data/ for the same
# reason, which is why LUA-008 and the loader composite fire too.
LUA-002 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/editor/sfframe.lua
LUA-002 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/instance.lua
LUA-002 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/libs_sh/builtins.lua
LUA-002 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/permissions/providers_sh/url_whitelist.lua
LUA-002 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/sflib.lua
LUA-003 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/libs_sh/builtins.lua
LUA-008 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/permissions/providers_sh/url_whitelist.lua
LUA-008 StarfallEx/lua/starfall/sflib.lua
COMP-010 StarfallEx

# Expression 2 and the Wire CPU are compilers with an editor attached. The E2
# test runner and the extension loader compile what they are given, and cpulib
# and the virtual machine assemble and run it.
LUA-002 wire/lua/entities/gmod_wire_expression2/core/e2tests.lua
LUA-002 wire/lua/entities/gmod_wire_expression2/core/extloader.lua
LUA-002 wire/lua/wire/cpulib.lua
LUA-002 wire/lua/wire/zvm/zvm_core.lua
LUA-008 wire/lua/entities/gmod_wire_expression2/core/e2tests.lua

# PAC3 builds outfit parts out of strings the wearer wrote: a command part, a
# script part, an expression library. Its data library serialises tables by
# compiling them back, and the editor reads and compiles tool files.
LUA-001 pac3/lua/pac3/editor/client/tools.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/autorun/pac_restart.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/core/client/parts/command.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/core/client/parts/script.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/editor/client/language.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/libraries/expression.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/libraries/luadata.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/libraries/webaudio.lua
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/libraries/webaudio/stream.lua
LUA-008 pac3/lua/autorun/pac_restart.lua
# PAC3's sound part defines its own local function called load and calls
# it twice. LUA-188 reads any load( as the chunk loader, which is right
# for a global and wrong here; one addon shadowing the name is the price.
LUA-188 pac3/lua/pac3/core/client/parts/sound.lua
LUA-008 pac3/lua/pac3/editor/client/language.lua
LUA-008 pac3/lua/pac3/editor/client/tools.lua

# PAC3 ships a file called test_suite_backdoor.lua which registers a net message
# that compiles and runs whatever is sent to it. It is their own test for exactly
# this, and the scanner is meant to shout about it.
LUA-002 pac3/lua/pac3/core/server/test_suite_backdoor.lua
LUA-013 pac3/lua/pac3/core/server/test_suite_backdoor.lua


# --- false-positive budget ---------------------------------------------------
#
# profile <rule> <max findings> <max files>
#
# Measured on this corpus, not guessed. Bumping the pinned commits changes
# these numbers, so regenerate them in the same commit that moves the pins.
#
# The CRITICAL lines above and the budgets below answer different questions.
# Up there: is this specific finding explained. Down here: has any rule started
# describing ordinary Lua rather than a technique.
#
# Two entries are worth naming as weak matches rather than techniques. OBF-001
# reads a long line with few spaces as packed source; here it catches a Starfall
# file that localises fifty functions on one line and a PAC3 table of sound
# paths. BIN-014 reads eighty base64 characters in a row as an embedded blob;
# here it catches a stretch of a bundled TrueType font. Two findings in 2690
# files is what these heuristics cost, and the budget is where that cost is
# written down rather than argued away.

# critical
profile LUA-002    25  19
profile LUA-008     7   6
profile LUA-001     6   5
profile LUA-121     3   1
profile COMP-010    1   1
profile LUA-003     1   1
profile LUA-188     3   1
profile LUA-013     1   1
profile LUA-040     1   1
profile LUA-044     1   1

